<article class="env-remark" id="07L6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07L6">Remark <span data-tag="07L6">60.17.6</span>.</a> The equivalence of Proposition <a href="/tag/07JH" data-tag="07JH">60.17.4</a> holds if we start with a surjection $P \to C$ where $P/A$ satisfies the strong lifting property of Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/07K4" data-tag="07K4">10.138.17</a>. To prove this we can argue as in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/07L5" data-tag="07L5">60.17.5</a>. (Details will be added here if we ever need this.) Presumably there is also a direct proof of this result, but the advantage of using polynomial rings is that the rings $D(n)$ are $p$-adic completions of divided power polynomial rings and the algebra is simplified. </p>
